"# F1 Unit Specs — batch 1\n\n2026-09-29 · @Mohammad AlFalasi\n\nEight unit specs are drafted on template v3 to the F1.17 standard: F1.1–F1.4, F1.14, F1.15, F1.16 and F1.18. Each unit has its own tab, with the spec table, the ten template-v3 sections, 3–8 open objects read at source, and a fact-check list for G3. Together they reach all nine world chapters, but unevenly: Europe appears in all eight, Australia and the Pacific in three, and the steppe only in F1.3, so batch 2 has to carry the steppe.\n\n## The eight units\n\n| Unit | Depth | Cases | Open objects read at source | Still to verify |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1.1 One object, three readings | O | The proto-cuneiform tablet (WS-001, passes the 1970 test with a caveat); an Iznik mosque lamp (Cleveland 1944.236); a Thonet chair (AIC 1999.556), where the access lens goes | 6 (Met, Cleveland, AIC) | The \"priest-king\" wording; Thonet dates; the tablet's exit from Iraq is unrecorded |\n",
"| F1.2 The reference-to-appropriation plane | O | Six sorting cases across five worlds, borrowing in both directions: a Congolese ivory souvenir; a Chinese export charger after a French print; a Company-school painting; the Navajo Nation v. Urban Outfitters case; the Tlahuitoltepec blouse and Isabel Marant (2015); a Grand Tour view | 5 (Cleveland) | The Tlahuitoltepec community's own statement, read only in press reports |\n| F1.3 How histories get written | P | Vasari's Lives; the Great Zimbabwe myth and Rhodesian censorship; \"Islamic art\" as a collectors' category | 2 plus a public-domain site plan | Mirabeau's date (1756 or 1757); exact Graeber and Wengrow wording |\n| F1.4 Film, performance, sound, games | P | Four National Jukebox recordings, 1903–1917 (blacksmith scene, Livery Stable Blues, Uncle Josh, Caruso); gamelan; kora; a Noh mask; a Westinghouse film; the Royal Game of Ur rules | 5 open records plus four public-domain recordings | Jukebox item pages refused access, so dates and matrix numbers come from its search data; possible caricature in two recordings |\n",
"| F1.14 Copying, transfer and counterfeit | P | Dürer and Marcantonio, as Vasari tells it; blue-and-white copied both ways (Jingdezhen, Iznik, Delft, Meissen); Indian chintz, the Calico Acts and Lancashire; training data as copying | 6, including a before/after pair: Dürer (AIC 1927.2897) and Marcantonio's copy (Met 17.3.3311) | The Getty v. Stability appeal; no open \"Made in Occupied Japan\" object |\n| F1.15 Ornament and the exhibition | P | A Damascus bowl once published as \"Venetian\"; Owen Jones's unnamed Tongan designer, paid in cloth; a Kuba raffia cloth | 3 (Cleveland) plus the 1856 Grammar of Ornament | Eglash left out (review only read); whether \"race\" appears in the 1851 catalogue |\n| F1.16 Ritual and belief | P | The One Million Pagodas and the Diamond Sutra (868); a Kongo crucifix, with both readings of the mission; an Andean kero | 6 (Met, Cleveland) | A pilgrim badge's pre-1970 history |\n",
"| F1.18 Women's making | P | A Girsu tablet listing 4,272 women and 1,800 children weaving (Ur III); May Morris's Honeysuckle credited to her father; a Maria Martinez plate signed \"Marie and Santana\" | 5 (AIC, Cleveland, Met) | Whether the Ur III weavers were free or unfree (probable only) |\n\n## What the protocol screen removed\n\nThe screen ran before any searching, and it took things out of four units. F1.15 dropped an object Owen Jones drew from, which is a preserved Māori head. F1.16 excluded minkisi, mallqui and huaca objects, katsina masks, restricted Aboriginal designs, Sámi drums, Ghost Dance garments, reliquaries and tabots. F1.4 excluded Okina masks. F1.2 carries a content note on the Congo ivory, since the trade rested on forced porterage. Tongan, Māori and ni-Vanuatu wording waits for a reader from each community before it is published.\n\n## Before G3\n\n- **Fact-check.** Every tab ends with its list: the R1 and R2 canon rows used, living people, community names, UNESCO claims and licences. The first two rounds show these rows err at 8–14% when drafted from memory.\n",
"- **Length.** The specs run 3,000–3,400 words against a 3,000 target, most of it URLs. The learner-facing copy is far shorter; the spec is the build brief.\n- **Next batches.** Batch 2 is F1.19, F1.19a, F1.19b (if Q36 is agreed), F1.20–F1.22a, F1.23 and F1.24. Batch 3 is F1.25–F1.31, with F1.28a and F1.30a. The partner-voice packs follow batch 1 (Q41).\n"
